= Solution
{parent=i}

Two <integrations by parts> give the boundary form of the <Sturm-Liouville operator>:
$$
\langle f,Lg\rangle-\langle Lf,g\rangle=\left[p\left(f'^*g-f^*g'\right)\right]_a^b.
$$
The interior terms cancel because $p,q$ are real. Thus the required endpoint condition is
$$
\boxed{\left[p(f'^*g-f^*g')\right]_a^b=0\quad\text{for every }f,g\text{ in the operator domain}.}
$$
For a regular interval, a standard <self-adjoint> domain is specified by one real homogeneous <Robin boundary condition> at each endpoint:
$$
A_a y(a)+B_a p(a)y'(a)=0,\qquad A_b y(b)+B_b p(b)y'(b)=0,
$$
where neither pair of real constants is identically zero. At each end, these conditions force the two boundary data vectors of $f,g$ to be proportional, making the displayed form zero. <Dirichlet boundary conditions> and <Neumann boundary conditions> are the cases $B=0$ and $A=0$. Coupled conditions also work, for example $y(a)=y(b)$ and $p(a)y'(a)=p(b)y'(b)$, whose two endpoint contributions cancel. Self-adjointness uses the maximal common domain with these conditions, not an arbitrary smaller symmetric restriction.

The <weighted inner product> must be nondegenerate. The usual interpretation is $w>0$ almost everywhere, allowing isolated endpoint zeros as in part (b), with the operator defined on its appropriate weighted domain. Merely allowing a weight that is zero on an entire interval would not by itself define the stated operator or a positive-definite <inner product> on ordinary functions.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $L\phi=\lambda\phi$ with $\phi\ne0$ in the <self-adjoint> domain. By the boundary identity from part (i),
$$
\lambda\langle\phi,\phi\rangle=\langle\phi,L\phi\rangle=\langle L\phi,\phi\rangle=\lambda^*\langle\phi,\phi\rangle.
$$
Since its squared weighted <norm> is positive, $\boxed{\lambda\in\mathbb R}$.

For $L\phi_n=\lambda_n\phi_n$ and $L\phi_m=\lambda_m\phi_m$, the same identity gives
$$
\lambda_n\langle\phi_m,\phi_n\rangle=\lambda_m\langle\phi_m,\phi_n\rangle.
$$
Thus $\lambda_n\ne\lambda_m$ implies
$$
\boxed{\int_a^b\phi_m^*(x)\phi_n(x)w(x)\,dx=0.}
$$
This is <orthogonality of Sturm-Liouville eigenfunctions>. Degenerate <eigenspaces> can be given an <orthonormal basis>, although vectors in a degenerate <eigenspace> are not automatically orthogonal.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Choose a complete orthonormal system of <eigenfunctions> $\phi_n$ for the <self-adjoint> problem. Expand $f=\sum_n f_n\phi_n$ and $y=\sum_n c_n\phi_n$, with
$$
f_n=\langle\phi_n,f\rangle=\int_a^b\phi_n^*(\xi)f(\xi)w(\xi)\,d\xi.
$$
Substitution into $Ly=f$ gives $\lambda_nc_n=f_n$. Since no <eigenvalue> is zero, the <Sturm-Liouville eigenfunction expansion> is
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\sum_n\frac{\phi_n(x)}{\lambda_n}\int_a^b\phi_n^*(\xi)f(\xi)w(\xi)\,d\xi.}
$$
For <eigenfunctions> not normalized to one, divide each term additionally by $\langle\phi_n,\phi_n\rangle$. The expansion is understood in the weighted function space and, with sufficient data regularity, in the operator domain. A zero <eigenvalue> would instead require its corresponding source coefficient to vanish and would leave an arbitrary homogeneous component.

