= Solution
{parent=a}

For the real decaying Bessel form used here, take $k>0$. The original PDF has $K_m(r)$ as the last factor in its auxiliary differential equation, although both differentiated factors have argument $kr$; that last argument must also be $kr$ for the stated <modified Bessel differential equation>.

Put $B(r)=K_m(kr)$. The corrected identity is $B''=-B'/r+(k^2+m^2/r^2)B$. Since the field is radial, the <radial Laplacian> gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
(\Delta-k^2)(r^{-p}B)
&=r^{-p}\left[B''+\frac{2-2p}{r}B'+\left(\frac{p(p-1)}{r^2}-k^2\right)B\right]\\
&=r^{-p}\left[\frac{1-2p}{r}B'+\frac{p(p-1)+m^2}{r^2}B\right].
\end{aligned}
$$
Away from the point source this must vanish. For $k>0$, a constant ratio of $rB'$ to $B$ would make $B$ a pure power, incompatible with the $k^2r^2$ term of its equation. Therefore both displayed coefficients must vanish:
$$
\boxed{p=\frac12,\qquad m^2=\frac14,\quad\text{so }m=\pm\frac12.}
$$
The two orders give the same <modified Bessel function of the second kind>: $K_{-1/2}(z)=K_{1/2}(z)=\sqrt{\pi/(2z)}e^{-z}$.<NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions>[§10.39, equation 10.39.2, equality of the two half-integer orders and their elementary form]{ref}

The source describes $k$ merely as real, but the following displayed real square-root formula implicitly needs $k>0$. For the operator with arbitrary real $k$, its decaying fundamental solution instead uses $\kappa=|k|$; $k=0$ is obtained by a limit. The unmodified real $A K_m(kr)$ ansatz is not a real Bessel formula for all negative $k$.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $C=A\sqrt{\pi/(2k)}$, so the radial field is $\Phi=C e^{-kr}/r$. Integrate its equation over a ball of radius $\epsilon$ centred at the source. The <divergence theorem> gives
$$
4\pi\epsilon^2\Phi'(\epsilon)-k^2\int_{r<\epsilon}\Phi\,dV=1.
$$
The second term is $4\pi k^2C\int_0^\epsilon re^{-kr}dr=O(\epsilon^2)$ and vanishes in the limit. Meanwhile
$$
4\pi\epsilon^2\Phi'(\epsilon)=-4\pi C(1+k\epsilon)e^{-k\epsilon}\longrightarrow-4\pi C.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{C=-\frac1{4\pi},\qquad A=-\frac1{4\pi}\sqrt{\frac{2k}{\pi}},\qquad\Phi(r)=-\frac{e^{-kr}}{4\pi r}.}
$$
The minus sign follows from the convention $(\Delta-k^2)\Phi=\delta^{(3)}$, opposite to the positive <static Yukawa Green function> for $-\Delta+k^2$. For arbitrary real $k$, replace it in the exponential by $|k|$ to select decay at infinity. The $k=0$ limit is $-1/(4\pi r)$.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Reflect the source across the plane, defining $\mathbf r'^*=(x',y',-z')$ for an interior point $z'>0$. Superpose the direct <Green function> from part (b) and an image of opposite sign:
$$
\boxed{\Psi(\mathbf r,\mathbf r')=-\frac1{4\pi}\left[\frac{e^{-\kappa|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|}}{|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|}-\frac{e^{-\kappa|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'^*|}}{|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'^*|}\right],\qquad\kappa=|k|.}
$$
The direct term supplies the delta source in the upper half-space; the image singularity lies outside that domain and contributes no source there. On $z=0$ the two distances are equal, so the terms cancel, giving homogeneous <Dirichlet boundary conditions>. Both decay at infinity, which is the additional condition selecting this half-space solution. This is the <method of images> realization of the <Dirichlet image kernel for the three-dimensional modified Helmholtz equation>. It includes the unscreened case $k=0$.

