= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $f=u+iv$ be analytic on a neighbourhood of a bounded planar region $\Omega$ and its piecewise smooth boundary $C$, oriented with $\Omega$ on the left. The <Cauchy integral theorem> states
$$
\boxed{\oint_Cf(z)\,dz=0.}
$$
For a region with holes, $C$ includes the clockwise inner boundaries as well as the counterclockwise outer one. In particular, the integral around a closed contour bounding a region throughout which $f$ is analytic is zero.

To prove this classical form, expand the line integral and apply <Green's theorem> to its real and imaginary parts:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\oint_C f\,dz
&=\oint_C(u\,dx-v\,dy)+i\oint_C(v\,dx+u\,dy)\\
&=\iint_\Omega(-v_x-u_y)\,dx\,dy+i\iint_\Omega(u_x-v_y)\,dx\,dy.
\end{aligned}
$$
Both integrands vanish by the <Cauchy-Riemann equations>, $u_x=v_y$ and $u_y=-v_x$. Analyticity supplies the continuous first partial derivatives needed by this <Green's theorem> proof. The requirement that the enclosed region contain no singularities is essential.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $s=1/n$, so $0<s<1$. For the <complex exponentiation> $z^s$, choose $-\pi/2<\arg z<3\pi/2$: the <branch cut> is the negative imaginary axis. Integrate $z^s/(a^2+z^2)$ around the upper half-plane semicircle, with a small semicircular indentation excluding the origin. The only enclosed <simple pole> is $z=ia$, with <residue>
$$
\operatorname{Res}_{z=ia}\frac{z^s}{a^2+z^2}=\frac{(ia)^s}{2ia}.
$$
The large arc is $O(R^{s-1})$ and the small arc is $O(\epsilon^{s+1})$, so both vanish. On the negative real segment, $z^s=e^{i\pi s}|z|^s$; accounting for the direction of traversal, the two real segments therefore sum to $(1+e^{i\pi s})I$, where $I$ is the desired positive-axis integral. The <residue theorem> gives
$$
(1+e^{i\pi s})I=\pi a^{s-1}e^{i\pi s/2}.
$$
Since $1+e^{i\pi s}=2e^{i\pi s/2}\cos(\pi s/2)$,
$$
\boxed{I=\frac{\pi a^{1/n-1}}{2\cos[\pi/(2n)]}.}
$$
The endpoint estimates also prove convergence. More generally the same calculation is the <fractional-power integral from an upper-half-plane contour>, valid for $-1<s<1$.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Set $z=e^{i\theta}$. Then $d\theta=dz/(iz)$ and $\tan\theta=(z^2-1)/[i(z^2+1)]$, so the requested integral is
$$
I=\frac1i\oint_{|z|=1}f(z)\,dz,\qquad f(z)=\frac{(1+z^2)^2}{z[a^2(1+z^2)^2-(1-z^2)^2]}.
$$
The denominator factors as
$$
[(a-1)+(a+1)z^2][(a+1)+(a-1)z^2].
$$
For $0<a<1$, its two inner roots are $z=\pm r$, $r=\sqrt{(1-a)/(1+a)}<1$, and the other two roots have modulus $1/r>1$. There is also a <simple pole> at zero. Their <residues> are
$$
\operatorname{Res}_{0}f=\frac1{a^2-1},\qquad\operatorname{Res}_{\pm r}f=\frac1{2a(1-a^2)}.
$$
The latter values follow by differentiating the quadratic product, or from the supplied hint. The sum of the enclosed <residues> is
$$
-\frac1{1-a^2}+\frac1{a(1-a^2)}=\frac1{a(1+a)}.
$$
The <residue theorem> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{I=\frac{2\pi}{a(1+a)},\qquad\mathcal C=2\pi.}
$$
At the angles where the tangent is undefined, the original reciprocal has a removable zero limit. Its continuous extension gives the same integral; the transformed rational integrand has no <pole> on the <unit circle>.

