= Solution
{parent=i}

With the stated <Fourier transform> convention, the exponential transform is a shifted <Dirac delta distribution>:
$$
\boxed{\widetilde f_1(\omega)=2\pi\delta(\omega-\omega_0).}
$$
Writing the sine as $(e^{i\omega_0t}-e^{-i\omega_0t})/(2i)$ then gives
$$
\boxed{\widetilde f_2(\omega)=\frac\pi i[\delta(\omega-\omega_0)-\delta(\omega+\omega_0)].}
$$
These are <tempered distributions>, since the time-domain functions are not integrable on the full real line. The second formula also correctly vanishes when $\omega_0=0$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

By the definition of <convolution> and <Fourier inversion>,
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal F^{-1}(\widetilde f*\widetilde g)(t)
&=\frac1{2\pi}\int_{\mathbb R}\int_{\mathbb R}\widetilde f(\eta)\widetilde g(\omega-\eta)e^{i\omega t}\,d\eta\,d\omega\\
&=\frac1{2\pi}\left[\int_{\mathbb R}\widetilde f(\eta)e^{i\eta t}d\eta\right]\left[\int_{\mathbb R}\widetilde g(\nu)e^{i\nu t}d\nu\right]\\
&=\boxed{2\pi f(t)g(t)}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The second line uses $\nu=\omega-\eta$. The factor $2\pi$ comes from the inverse-transform normalization. This is the product form of the <convolution theorem>, initially justified for sufficiently decaying functions and extended when the indicated products and convolutions exist.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The reciprocal in this transform must mean the <principal-value reciprocal distribution>. Its inverse transform is
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal F^{-1}\!\left(\operatorname{PV}\frac1\omega\right)(t)
&=\frac{i}{\pi}\int_0^\infty\frac{\sin(\omega t)}\omega\,d\omega\\
&=\frac i2\operatorname{sgn}t,
\end{aligned}
$$
using the given <Dirichlet integral> and the sign of $t$. Also $\mathcal F^{-1}[\delta](t)=1/(2\pi)$. The inverse of the proposed expression is consequently $(iA/2)\operatorname{sgn}t+B/(2\pi)$. Comparison with $H(t)=[1+\operatorname{sgn}t]/2$ for $t\ne0$ gives
$$
\boxed{A=-i,\qquad B=\pi,\qquad\widetilde H(\omega)=-i\operatorname{PV}\frac1\omega+\pi\delta(\omega).}
$$
The value assigned at the single point $t=0$ does not affect the distribution. This is the <Fourier transform of the Heaviside step function>; treating $1/\omega$ as an ordinary integral would miss the required prescription.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Multiply $H$ by the exponential form of the sine and use the <modulation property of the Fourier transform>:
$$
\widetilde f(\omega)=\frac1{2i}[\widetilde H(\omega-\omega_0)-\widetilde H(\omega+\omega_0)].
$$
Inserting part (iii) gives
$$
\boxed{\widetilde f(\omega)=\frac12\left[\operatorname{PV}\frac1{\omega+\omega_0}-\operatorname{PV}\frac1{\omega-\omega_0}\right]+\frac\pi{2i}[\delta(\omega-\omega_0)-\delta(\omega+\omega_0)].}
$$
The reciprocal terms are real principal-value distributions and the delta terms are imaginary. Equivalently, exponential damping $H(t)e^{-\epsilon t}$ gives $\omega_0/[(\epsilon+i\omega)^2+\omega_0^2]$, whose distributional limit is the boxed expression. The shifted-difference formula remains valid at $\omega_0=0$, where all terms cancel.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Transform only the time variable. Two <integrations by parts> give $\mathcal F_t[y_{tt}]=(i\omega)^2\widetilde y=-\omega^2\widetilde y$, when the ordinary transform boundary terms vanish; the same derivative rule holds for <tempered distributions>. Spatial derivatives and the fixed spatial delta commute with the transform. Thus
$$
-\omega^2\widetilde y=\widetilde y_{xx}+\widetilde F(\omega)\delta(x-x_0),
$$
or
$$
\boxed{\widetilde y_{xx}=-\omega^2\widetilde y-\widetilde F(\omega)\delta(x-x_0).}
$$
The spatial <Dirichlet boundary conditions> transform to $\widetilde y(\omega,0)=\widetilde y(\omega,L)=0$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Multiply the transformed equation by $\sin(k_nx)$ and integrate from zero to $L$. Two <integrations by parts>, using the zero endpoint values of both $\widetilde y$ and the sine, give
$$
\int_0^L\widetilde y_{xx}\sin(k_nx)dx=-k_n^2\int_0^L\widetilde y\sin(k_nx)dx.
$$
The delta source contributes $\widetilde F(\omega)\sin(k_nx_0)$. Multiplying by $2/L$ and using the coefficient definition therefore yields
$$
(k_n^2-\omega^2)b_n(\omega)=\frac2L\sin(k_nx_0)\widetilde F(\omega).
$$
Away from the resonant frequencies this gives
$$
\boxed{b_n(\omega)=\alpha_n\frac{\widetilde F(\omega)}{k_n^2-\omega^2},\qquad\alpha_n=\frac2L\sin(k_nx_0).}
$$
At $\omega=\pm k_n$ the quotient must be interpreted with a <pole> prescription if the source excites that mode. In distributional form, arbitrary delta terms at those frequencies may also be added; they are the free homogeneous oscillations. This is the frequency-space issue addressed explicitly in part (iii).

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Expand in the complete <Fourier sine series> $\widetilde y(\omega,x)=\sum_{n\geq1}b_n(\omega)\sin(k_nx)$ and apply <Fourier inversion>. Substitution of part (ii) gives
$$
\boxed{y(t,x)=\frac1{\pi L}\sum_{n=1}^\infty\sin(k_nx_0)\sin(k_nx)\int_{-\infty}^\infty\frac{\widetilde F(\omega)e^{i\omega t}}{k_n^2-\omega^2}\,d\omega,\qquad C=\frac1{\pi L}.}
$$
To check the source normalization, apply $\partial_t^2-\partial_x^2$ mode by mode. It cancels the denominator; the remaining inverse transform gives $2\pi F(t)$, and completeness gives
$$
\frac2L\sum_{n\geq1}\sin(k_nx_0)\sin(k_nx)=\delta(x-x_0).
$$
The endpoints vanish termwise. These identities establish the equation in the distributional sense, with classical validity wherever the series and derivatives converge suitably.

The displayed real-axis integrals in the paper are not ordinary convergent integrals at their <poles>. One definite choice is the <Cauchy principal value> at each $\omega=\pm k_n$. If a causal solution is wanted, use $[k_n^2-(\omega-i0)^2]^{-1}$ instead. Its inverse transform is $H(t)\sin(k_nt)/k_n$, so the causal solution is
$$
y_{\rm ret}(t,x)=\frac2L\sum_{n\geq1}\frac{\sin(k_nx_0)\sin(k_nx)}{k_n}\int_{-\infty}^tF(s)\sin[k_n(t-s)]\,ds,
$$
when the past integral exists. The principal-value choice instead uses the time-symmetric kernel $\sin(k_n|t|)/(2k_n)$. No temporal initial data are given, so homogeneous sine and cosine modes remain permissible additions. This is the <causal Fourier resolvent of a point-driven fixed string>.

= 6
