= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Under the specified passive change of <orthonormal basis>, each Cartesian <tensor> index acquires one copy of $L$. Thus a <tensor> of order $n$ transforms as
$$
\boxed{S'_{a_1\cdots a_n}=L_{a_1b_1}\cdots L_{a_nb_n}S_{b_1\cdots b_n}.}
$$
Repeated old-basis indices are summed from one to three. <Orthogonality> of the new basis gives $LL^T=I$. Taking <determinants> yields $(\det L)^2=1$, hence $\boxed{\det L=+1\text{ or }-1}$. The positive case preserves orientation and the negative case reverses it.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A <pseudotensor> has an additional <determinant> factor under an orthogonal transformation. Thus its rank-three transformation law is
$$
\epsilon'_{ijk}=(\det L)L_{ia}L_{jb}L_{kc}\epsilon_{abc}.
$$
The contracted expression $L_{ia}L_{jb}L_{kc}\epsilon_{abc}$ is fully antisymmetric in $i,j,k$, so it equals a scalar multiple of $\epsilon_{ijk}$. For $(i,j,k)=(1,2,3)$ it is precisely the <determinant> expansion of $L$, fixing that scalar to $\det L$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\epsilon'_{ijk}=(\det L)^2\epsilon_{ijk}=\epsilon_{ijk}.}
$$
The <Levi-Civita symbol> consequently has the same numerical entries in every orthonormal frame when interpreted as a pseudotensor. With an ordinary rank-three <tensor> transformation law alone it would instead acquire a minus sign under an orientation-reversing transformation.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Integrating the <Dirac delta distribution> in $x_3$ reduces the inertia integral to the planar lamina. Use $u=x_1-x_2$, $v=x_2$, so $x_1=u+v$ and the parallelogram becomes the square $-D\leq u,v\leq D$, with unit <Jacobian determinant>. The mass is $M=4\sigma D^2$, and symmetry gives a <centre of mass> at the origin.

Odd rectangular integrals vanish. The required second moments are
$$
\begin{aligned}
\int x_1^2dm&=\sigma\int_{-D}^D\!\int_{-D}^D(u+v)^2du\,dv=\frac83\sigma D^4,\\
\int x_2^2dm&=\sigma\int_{-D}^D\!\int_{-D}^D v^2du\,dv=\frac43\sigma D^4,\\
\int x_1x_2dm&=\sigma\int_{-D}^D\!\int_{-D}^D(u+v)v\,du\,dv=\frac43\sigma D^4.
\end{aligned}
$$
The mixed moments involving $x_3$ vanish. Hence the <inertia tensor> is
$$
\boxed{I=\frac{4\sigma D^4}{3}\begin{pmatrix}1&-1&0\\-1&2&0\\0&0&3\end{pmatrix}=\frac{MD^2}{3}\begin{pmatrix}1&-1&0\\-1&2&0\\0&0&3\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
The off-diagonal entries are minus the product moment, not the product moment itself. This is the unit-shear case of the <inertia tensor of a sheared rectangular lamina>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Put $J_0=4\sigma D^4/3=MD^2/3$. The normal to the lamina is already an <eigenvector>, with moment $3J_0$. The planar block has <characteristic polynomial>
$$
\det\begin{pmatrix}1-\lambda&-1\\-1&2-\lambda\end{pmatrix}=\lambda^2-3\lambda+1,
$$
so the <principal moments of inertia> are
$$
\boxed{I_-=\frac{J_0}{2}(3-\sqrt5),\qquad I_+=\frac{J_0}{2}(3+\sqrt5),\qquad I_3=3J_0.}
$$
A corresponding orthonormal set of <principal axes> is
$$
\boxed{\mathbf n_-=\frac{(2,\sqrt5-1,0)}{\sqrt{10-2\sqrt5}},\qquad\mathbf n_+=\frac{(2,-1-\sqrt5,0)}{\sqrt{10+2\sqrt5}},\qquad\mathbf n_3=(0,0,1).}
$$
The sign of each axis vector is immaterial. The smaller-moment axis makes angle $\tfrac12\arctan(2)$ with the positive $x_1$ direction, approximately $31.7^\circ$; the other planar axis is perpendicular. As a check, $I_-+I_+=3J_0=I_3$, the <perpendicular axis theorem> for a planar lamina.

\Image[paper-2-lamina-principal-axes.png]
{title=The two planar <principal axes> of the sheared square lamina, with the third axis normal to the page}

= 7
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=7}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The four spring extensions are $x_1$, $x_2-x_1$, $x_3-x_2$ and $-x_3$. <Hooke's law> therefore gives <potential energy> $k[x_1^2+(x_2-x_1)^2+(x_3-x_2)^2+x_3^2]/2$, while the <kinetic energy> is $m\sum_i\dot x_i^2/2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{L=\frac m2\sum_{i=1}^3\dot x_i^2-\frac k2[x_1^2+(x_2-x_1)^2+(x_3-x_2)^2+x_3^2],}
$$
with the matrices in the requested <quadratic form>
$$
\boxed{T=mI_3,\qquad V=k\begin{pmatrix}2&-1&0\\-1&2&-1\\0&-1&2\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
The equilibrium offsets disappear because the expansion is in displacements from static equilibrium.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Write $d=2k-m\omega^2$. The characteristic <determinant> of the tridiagonal matrix is
$$
\det(V-\omega^2T)=d(d^2-k^2)-k^2d=d(d^2-2k^2).
$$
The three values of $d$ are $\sqrt2k$, $0$ and $-\sqrt2k$. Therefore the <normal mode> frequencies and convenient Euclidean-normalized generalized <eigenvectors> are
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c}
\omega^2&\mathbf u\\\hline
(2-\sqrt2)k/m&(1,\sqrt2,1)^T/2\\
2k/m&(1,0,-1)^T/\sqrt2\\
(2+\sqrt2)k/m&(1,-\sqrt2,1)^T/2
\end{array}.}
$$
Each pair satisfies $V\mathbf u=\omega^2T\mathbf u$. The first mode moves all masses in phase, the middle mode leaves the central mass fixed, and the last has the central mass moving opposite to the outer masses. All squared frequencies are positive, consistent with the positive spring potential and fixed endpoints.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Using the three real <eigenvectors> and positive frequencies in part (ii), the general motion is
$$
\boxed{\mathbf x(t)=\sum_{j=1}^3\mathbf u_j[A_j\cos(\omega_jt)+B_j\sin(\omega_jt)].}
$$
There are **six independent real constants**, $A_1,A_2,A_3,B_1,B_2,B_3$, corresponding to the three initial displacements and three initial velocities. With the displayed orthonormal <eigenvectors>, $A_j=\mathbf u_j^T\mathbf x(0)$ and $B_j=\mathbf u_j^T\dot{\mathbf x}(0)/\omega_j$. This spans all solutions because the normal-mode <eigenvectors> form a basis.

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Introduce the fixed endpoint values $x_0=x_{N+1}=0$. The <Lagrangian> is
$$
\boxed{L=\frac m2\sum_{j=1}^N\dot x_j^2-\frac k2\sum_{j=0}^N(x_{j+1}-x_j)^2.}
$$
Thus $T=mI_N$, $V_{ij}=k(2\delta_{ij}-\delta_{i,j+1}-\delta_{i,j-1})$, and
$$
\boxed{V-\omega^2T=\begin{pmatrix}d&-k&0&\cdots&0\\-k&d&-k&\ddots&\vdots\\0&-k&d&\ddots&0\\\vdots&\ddots&\ddots&\ddots&-k\\0&\cdots&0&-k&d\end{pmatrix},\qquad d=2k-m\omega^2.}
$$
This is a symmetric tridiagonal matrix, reflecting that only nearest-neighbour masses are coupled.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Expand the tridiagonal <determinant> along its first row. The diagonal entry contributes $dD_{N-1}$. In the off-diagonal cofactor, expansion along its first column supplies the other factor $-k$, and the cofactor sign makes the net contribution $-k^2D_{N-2}$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{D_N=dD_{N-1}-k^2D_{N-2},\qquad N\geq2.}
$$
The initial values are $D_1=d$ and $D_2=d^2-k^2$. Writing the same formula at $N=2$ forces $\boxed{D_0=1}$, the empty <determinant> convention. This is the <tridiagonal determinant recurrence>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Substitute $D_N=\beta^N$ into the recurrence. Since $k>0$, its roots are nonzero and satisfy
$$
\beta^2-d\beta+k^2=0,\qquad\boxed{\beta_1+\beta_2=d,\quad\beta_1\beta_2=k^2.}
$$
For distinct roots, the general recurrence solution is $D_N=c_1\beta_1^N+c_2\beta_2^N$. The initial values require $c_1+c_2=1$ and $c_1\beta_1+c_2\beta_2=d=\beta_1+\beta_2$, giving
$$
\boxed{c_1=\frac{\beta_1}{\beta_1-\beta_2},\qquad c_2=-\frac{\beta_2}{\beta_1-\beta_2},\qquad D_N=\frac{\beta_1^{N+1}-\beta_2^{N+1}}{\beta_1-\beta_2}.}
$$
At a repeated root, $d=\pm2k$ and $\beta_1=\beta_2=\beta=\pm k$, this quotient must be replaced by its limit:
$$
\boxed{D_N=(N+1)\beta^N.}
$$
It also follows by solving the repeated-root recurrence as $(c_1+c_2N)\beta^N$. This exceptional case is necessary to avoid introducing a false root in the next part; it is included in the <fixed-end spring-chain determinant and its repeated-root limit>.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For distinct nonzero recurrence roots, the <determinant> formula gives
$$
\boxed{D_N=0\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\left(\frac{\beta_1}{\beta_2}\right)^{N+1}=1,\qquad\beta_1\ne\beta_2.}
$$
The ratio one is excluded: at that repeated root, part (iii) gives $(N+1)(\pm k)^N\ne0$. Thus the naive extra root corresponding to equal recurrence roots is spurious.

To count the physical solutions without ambiguity from exchanging $\beta_1$ and $\beta_2$, write $\beta_{1,2}=ke^{\pm iq}$ and $d=2k\cos q$ with $0<q<\pi$. Then
$$
D_N=k^N\frac{\sin[(N+1)q]}{\sin q}.
$$
Its $N$ distinct zeros occur at $q=j\pi/(N+1)$, $j=1,\ldots,N$. They give **exactly $N$ distinct positive normal frequencies**, or equivalently $N$ distinct roots in $\omega^2$:
$$
\omega_j=2\sqrt{\frac km}\sin\frac{j\pi}{2(N+1)}.
$$
If signed $\omega$ is counted, the <characteristic equation> has $2N$ roots $\pm\omega_j$. The sine quotient is the <Chebyshev polynomial of the second kind> representation of the <fixed-end spring-chain determinant and its repeated-root limit>.

= 8
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $B(x)$ for the displayed matrix. The hyperbolic addition identities give
$$
B(x)B(y)=\begin{pmatrix}\cosh(x+y)&-\sinh(x+y)\\-\sinh(x+y)&\cosh(x+y)\end{pmatrix}=B(x+y).
$$
Thus <matrix multiplication> is closed in $G$. It is associative because ordinary <matrix multiplication> is associative. The identity is $B(0)=I$, and $B(x)B(-x)=I$ gives the inverse $B(x)^{-1}=B(-x)$. Hence $\boxed{G\text{ is a group}}$.

Moreover, $x\mapsto B(x)$ is an injective homomorphism from the additive real numbers, since $\sinh x$ determines $x$. Therefore $\boxed{G\cong(\mathbb R,+)}$; in particular it is abelian. These matrices are the connected boost <subgroup> relevant to the <component quotient of the real Lorentz group in two dimensions>.

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a <subgroup> $N\leq H$ and $h\in H$, a left <coset> is $hN=\{hn:n\in N\}$ and a right <coset> is $Nh=\{nh:n\in N\}$. A <normal subgroup> satisfies $hNh^{-1}=N$ for every $h\in H$, equivalently $hN=Nh$.

If $N$ is normal, the <quotient group> is the set $H/N$ of <cosets>, with multiplication
$$
\boxed{(hN)(kN)=(hk)N.}
$$
To check independence of representatives, replace $h,k$ by $hn_1,kn_2$. Then
$$
(hn_1)(kn_2)=hk(k^{-1}n_1k)n_2\in hkN,
$$
because normality puts $k^{-1}n_1k$ in $N$. Thus the operation is well defined. Associativity follows from that in $H$, the identity is $N$, and the inverse of $hN$ is $h^{-1}N$. This proves the group axioms for $H/N$ and explains the role of <coset multiplication is well-defined exactly for normal subgroups>.

= c
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Direct multiplication gives $T^2=P^2=I$, $TP=PT=-I$, and
$$
TB(x)T=PB(x)P=B(-x).
$$
Thus conjugation by each generator of $G'$ preserves $G$; so does conjugation by every product of the generators. Therefore $\boxed{G\trianglelefteq G'}$.

Using $TB(x)=B(-x)T$ and its analogue for $P$, every group word can be reduced to $B(x)S$ with $S\in\{I,T,P,TP\}$. Consequently the quotient has at most four <cosets>. They are distinct: a nonidentity diagonal matrix among $T,P,-I$ cannot equal $B(x)$, since a diagonal $B(x)$ requires $\sinh x=0$ and hence $B(x)=I$. Therefore
$$
G'/G=\{G,TG,PG,TPG\}.
$$
The three nonidentity <cosets> all square to $G$, and $TG$ and $PG$ commute because $T,P$ commute. Hence
$$
\boxed{G'/G\cong C_2\times C_2,\quad\text{the Klein four-group}.}
$$
Equivalently, $G'$ is the full real $O(1,1)$ group and $G$ its <identity component>. This is the <component quotient of the real Lorentz group in two dimensions>; the quotient records the four components, not the continuous boost parameter.

= d
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The <subgroup> generated by $T,P$ is $H=\{I,T,P,-I\}$, a <Klein four-group>. It is not normal in $G'$. Indeed,
$$
B(x)TB(-x)=B(2x)T=\begin{pmatrix}\cosh2x&\sinh2x\\-\sinh2x&-\cosh2x\end{pmatrix}.
$$
For $x\ne0$ the off-diagonal entries are nonzero, whereas every member of $H$ is diagonal. Thus this conjugate of $T$ is outside $H$, giving
$$
\boxed{\langle T,P\rangle\not\trianglelefteq G'.}
$$
The <normal subgroup> in part (c) is the continuous boost <subgroup>; choosing one representative from each of its <cosets> does not make those representatives a <normal subgroup>.

= 9
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The relation $mR=R^{-1}m$ moves every reflection factor to the right of every rotation factor. Canceling pairs $m^2=I$ and reducing rotation powers modulo $n$ shows that every word has one of the forms
$$
\boxed{D_n=\{R^j:0\leq j<n\}\ \cup\ \{R^jm:0\leq j<n\}.}
$$
Within either family the elements are distinct, since equality would imply equality of two powers of $R$, which has order $n$. No rotation equals a reflection: in the polygon action, rotations preserve planar orientation and reflections reverse it. Thus this complete list has exactly $2n$ elements.

Geometrically, $R$ rotates the polygon by $2\pi/n$ about its centre, and $m$ reflects it in a symmetry axis through the centre. The elements $R^j$ are all rotations; $R^jm$ are all reflections. We use the paper's convention that $D_n$ has order $2n$, since another common convention calls this same group $D_{2n}$.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Rotations commute with one another, while reflection conjugation sends $R^j$ to $R^{-j}$. Thus the rotational <conjugacy classes> are pairs $\{R^j,R^{-j}\}$, except when the two entries coincide. For reflections, direct use of the relations gives
$$
R^s(R^jm)R^{-s}=R^{j+2s}m,\qquad m(R^jm)m^{-1}=R^{-j}m.
$$
If $n$ is odd, multiplication by two permutes <residues> modulo $n$, so all reflections are conjugate. The complete class list is
$$
\boxed{\{I\};\quad\{R^j,R^{-j}\}\ (1\leq j\leq(n-1)/2);\quad\{R^jm:0\leq j<n\}.}
$$
Their sizes are $1$, then $(n-1)/2$ classes of size two, then one class of size $n$, giving $(n+3)/2$ classes and $2n$ elements in total.

If $n$ is even, the half-turn $R^{n/2}$ is central and forms its own singleton. Conjugation changes a reflection exponent by an even amount or negates it, so it preserves parity and reaches every exponent of that parity. The list is
$$
\boxed{\begin{gathered}\{I\},\quad\{R^{n/2}\};\qquad\{R^j,R^{-j}\}\ (1\leq j<n/2);\\
\{R^{2j}m:0\leq j<n/2\},\qquad\{R^{2j+1}m:0\leq j<n/2\}.
\end{gathered}}
$$
There are $n/2+3$ classes; their sizes are $1,1$, then $n/2-1$ pairs, then two classes of size $n/2$. Geometrically the two reflection classes correspond to vertex-axis and edge-axis reflections, in either order depending on the choice of $m$. These are the <conjugacy classes of a finite dihedral group>.

= c
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $n=p\geq3$ be prime. Any <subgroup> contained in $\langle R\rangle\cong C_p$ is either $\{I\}$ or the whole rotation <subgroup>. Both are normal: the trivial <subgroup> always is, and the rotation <subgroup> has index two, or equivalently is preserved by $mRm^{-1}=R^{-1}$.

If a <normal subgroup> contains any reflection, it contains its entire <conjugacy class>. Part (b) shows that this is every reflection for odd $p$. Products of reflections include a generator of the rotations, since $(Rm)m=R$. Such a <normal subgroup> must therefore be the whole dihedral group.

Thus the complete list of proper <normal subgroups>, including the trivial <subgroup>, is
$$
\boxed{\{I\}\quad\text{and}\quad\langle R\rangle=\{I,R,\ldots,R^{p-1}\}.}
$$
This also agrees with <classification of subgroups of a dihedral group>: the reflection <subgroups> of order two are proper but are not normal when $p$ is odd.

= 10
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Work over the complex numbers, the usual scalar field for the finite-group <character> theory used below. A <group representation> on a nonzero vector space $V$ is a homomorphism $D:G\to GL(V)$, so $D(gh)=D(g)D(h)$ and $D(e)=I$.

A <faithful representation> is injective: distinct group elements have distinct representing maps. Equivalently, its kernel is just $\{e\}$.

An <irreducible representation> has no nonzero proper invariant subspace: if $W\subseteq V$ and $D(g)W\subseteq W$ for every $g\in G$, then $W=\{0\}$ or $W=V$. Irreducibility concerns invariant subspaces, whereas faithfulness concerns whether any group element is represented by the identity.

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The homomorphism law and $gg^{-1}=g^{-1}g=e$ give
$$
D(g)D(g^{-1})=D(e)=I,\qquad D(g^{-1})D(g)=I.
$$
Thus $D(g^{-1})$ is both a left and right inverse of $D(g)$, proving
$$
\boxed{D(g^{-1})=[D(g)]^{-1}.}
$$
If only multiplicativity is initially stated, $D(e)^2=D(e)$ together with invertibility of representation matrices first implies $D(e)=I$. This explains why a <group representation> takes values in the <general linear group> rather than arbitrary square matrices.

= c
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The elements $1,-1$ commute with the whole <quaternion group>, so they form singleton classes. Each of the three remaining pairs is a <conjugacy class>: for example conjugation by $j$ sends $i$ to $-i$, and likewise cyclically. No such pair can mix with a different pair, because the quotient by $\{\pm1\}$ is abelian and its <cosets> $\{\pm i\}$, $\{\pm j\}$, $\{\pm k\}$ are preserved by conjugation. Hence
$$
\boxed{\{1\},\quad\{-1\},\quad\{i,-i\},\quad\{j,-j\},\quad\{k,-k\}.}
$$
For a finite group over $\mathbb C$, the number of inequivalent <irreducible representations> equals the number of <conjugacy classes>, so there are five. The <sum of squares of irreducible representation dimensions> is the <group order>, eight. With five positive integer dimensions, the only possibility is
$$
\boxed{1,1,1,1,2.}
$$
Indeed five one-dimensional contributions give five, and replacing one by a two-dimensional contribution adds exactly three; any further enlargement would exceed eight. These are the dimensions used in the <character table of the quaternion group>.

= d
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For a one-dimensional <character>, write $\chi(i)=\epsilon$ and $\chi(j)=\eta$. Since $\chi(-1)=1$, the relations $i^2=j^2=-1$ require $\epsilon,\eta\in\{\pm1\}$. Also $ij=k$ gives $\chi(k)=\epsilon\eta$. The four sign choices therefore supply all four linear characters.

For the remaining two-dimensional <character> $\chi_2$, its value at the identity is two. Column <orthogonality> between the identity and $-1$ gives $4+2\chi_2(-1)=0$, so $\chi_2(-1)=-2$. Between the identity and each of the other classes, the four linear-character values sum to zero, forcing the remaining <character> values to vanish. Thus the <character table> is
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
&\{1\}&\{-1\}&\{\pm i\}&\{\pm j\}&\{\pm k\}\\\hline
\text{class size}&1&1&2&2&2\\\hline
\chi_{++}&1&1&1&1&1\\
\chi_{+-}&1&1&1&-1&-1\\
\chi_{-+}&1&1&-1&1&-1\\
\chi_{--}&1&1&-1&-1&1\\
\chi_2&2&-2&0&0&0
\end{array}.}
$$
Here column <orthogonality> is the standard finite-group <character> theorem $\sum_\rho\chi_\rho(e)^*\chi_\rho(g)=0$ for $g\ne e$. The rows also have unit <norm> and are mutually orthogonal in the <character inner product> with the displayed class-size weights. This completes the <character table of the quaternion group>.

= e
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Taking traces of the given matrices gives the <character> on the five classes,
$$
\chi_D=(4,-4,0,0,0).
$$
For a complex finite-group representation, the multiplicity of an irrep $\rho$ is its <character inner product> with $\chi_D$:
$$
n_\rho=\frac1{|H|}\sum_{C}|C|\,\chi_\rho(C)^*\chi_D(C).
$$
Every linear <character> has value one at both $1$ and $-1$, so its multiplicity is $(4-4)/8=0$. For the two-dimensional <character>,
$$
n_2=\frac18[2\cdot4+(-2)(-4)]=2.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{D\cong\rho_2\oplus\rho_2,\qquad(n_{++},n_{+-},n_{-+},n_{--},n_2)=(0,0,0,0,2).}
$$
The dimensions check: $2\times2=4$.

The choice of scalar field matters here, even though the displayed matrices are real. Over $\mathbb R$, this particular four-dimensional representation is irreducible: for any nonzero real vector $v$, the vectors $v,D(i)v,D(j)v,D(k)v$ are mutually orthogonal with the same nonzero <norm>. This follows from the skew-symmetry and quaternion multiplication identities of the three matrices. A nonzero invariant real subspace contains all four vectors and is therefore the whole space. Its complexification is the two-copy decomposition above.
